=== Motivation ===

This proposal aims to enable the singleton pattern on Bitcoin without requiring nodes to validate external state or execute complex recursive covenants in script.

The singleton pattern entails an unbroken lineage of UTXOs descending from a genesis output, which can be inherited by at most one output per transaction. Proving this lineage efficiently is a challenge. Existing proposals require either external state (see [https://github.com/JohnLaw2/btc-iids/blob/main/iids14.pdf Inherited IDs]) or [https://gnusha.org/pi/bitcoindev/20220310064717.GA7597@erisian.com.au/ recursive introspection in script].

This proposal, in contrast, requires no external state, and the cost to validate descent is strictly O(1), regardless of ancestor size. This is achieved through a new output type that commits to a singleton identifier, which cryptographically proves unique descent from a genesis output.

This capability eliminates the [https://bitcoinops.org/en/newsletters/2025/01/10/#contract-level-relative-timelocks 2x-delay problem] in LN-Symmetry by enabling [https://delvingbitcoin.org/t/contract-level-relative-timelocks-or-lets-talk-about-ancestry-proofs-and-singletons/1353 contract-level relative timelocks]. In addition, it enables non-interactive bridging in future rollups like [https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/068 ShieldedCSV], avoiding HTLC swaps through conditional delegation.

To facilitate usage, a companion proposal [[bip-XXXX-op-singleton.md|OP_SINGLETON]] has been prepared, which exposes the identifier in script via cross-input introspection.

== Design ==

Pay-to-Singleton (P2SG) is a new output type that commits to a script tree Merkle root and a singleton identifier. It operates with nearly the same functionality as [[bip-0341.mediawiki|Pay-to-Taproot]] (P2TR), except there is no key-path spend and transactions may only create Singleton outputs when the following identifier invariants are provably preserved:<ref>'''Why is there no key-path spend?''' Unlike P2TR, P2SG is intended for complex L2 applications, which almost always need advanced scripting capabilities. In LN-Symmetry, for instance, cooperative closures remain P2TR spends (for privacy reasons). Adding an infrequently used key-path spend option would therefore introduce unnecessary complexity while increasing validation costs and the size of the typical witness.</ref>
* Multiple outputs in the UTXO set cannot commit to the same identifier simultaneously.
* An output that commits to an existing identifier spends its immediate predecessor.

To achieve this, the identifier commits to the value of the genesis output, the genesis output index, the initial script tree Merkle root, and the first prevout in the genesis transaction, which may not be a coinbase.<ref>'''How does the identifier provide proof of unique descent from genesis?''' In the genesis transaction, the identifier commits to the first prevout and the output index to ensure the identifier is cryptographically unique. The uniqueness of the first prevout is guaranteed by [[bip-0030.mediawiki|BIP 30]] and [[bip-0034.mediawiki|BIP 34]] and by forbidding coinbase genesis transactions, and the same-index continuation rule ensures that the identifier remains unique in subsequent transactions.</ref><ref>'''Why does the identifier commit to the initial script tree Merkle root and output value?''' The identifier commits to the initial script tree Merkle root and output value in order to prevent theft via chain reorganizations. If users add funds to an L2, for instance, by committing to its identifier, theft would be possible if the initial conditions of the L2 could change via a reorg while the identifier remains the same. For example, fake depositors could be added, the initial balance could be reclaimed, or the unlocking conditions could be changed entirely. Committing to the full set of initial conditions eliminates this attack vector.</ref>

The identifier commitment uses a tagged hash as introduced by [[bip-0340.mediawiki|BIP 340]] and [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP 341]], using the tag ''SingletonIdentifier'':

<source>
p = prevout of the first input in the genesis transaction (32-byte hash + 4-byte little-endian)
n = output index (4-byte, little endian)
v = initial output value (8-byte, little endian)
r = initial script tree Merkle root (32-byte)
identifier = tagged_hash("SingletonIdentifier", p || n || v || r)
</source>

The output hash commits to the identifier and the current script tree Merkle root, using the tag ''SingletonHash'':

<source>
i = identifier (32-byte)
r = current script tree Merkle root (32-byte)
output hash = tagged_hash("SingletonHash", i || r)
</source>

The script tree Merkle root is defined as in [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP 341]], with each branch hashed with the tag "TapBranch" and each leaf hashed with the tag "TapLeaf."

When a Singleton output is spent, the identifier may be inherited by at most one output, which must appear at the same index in the transaction. A Singleton output created via inheritance is called a "continuation," whereas one created without inheritance is called a "genesis."

Transactions are forbidden from creating a Singleton output without proof of genesis or continuation:
* To provide proof of genesis, the transaction must not be a coinbase and the next output must be an <code>OP_RETURN</code> that publishes the initial script tree Merkle root, proving that the genesis output commits to the derived identifier.<ref>'''Why can the genesis transaction not be a coinbase?''' The singleton identifier derives entropy from the prevout at the first index, which is not unique in coinbase transactions. While adding support for coinbase transactions is possible, it is not worth the implementation complexity given the lack of a compelling use case.</ref><ref>'''Why is an <code>OP_RETURN</code> used to publish the initial script tree Merkle root?''' The cleanest alternative would be a new SegWit version with an output witness field, but this is invasive and hard to justify. Within existing SegWit, the <code>OP_RETURN</code> is necessary because [[bip-0141.mediawiki|BIP 141]] caps witness programs at 40 bytes and two 20-byte hashes provide insufficient security. An annex could in principle be used (e.g., by looking for a tagged annex at the same index), but an <code>OP_RETURN</code> fallback would still be necessary for transactions without a v1+ Segwit input. Given this, in an effort to minimize complexity, an <code>OP_RETURN</code>-only solution is considered the best option.</ref><ref>'''Why must the genesis <code>OP_RETURN</code> immediately follow the genesis output?''' The genesis <code>OP_RETURN</code> immediately follows the genesis output so that output validation is O(1), parallelizable, and straightforward to implement. In contrast, combining all genesis <code>OP_RETURN</code> data into a single <code>OP_RETURN</code> would make it hard to validate outputs in isolation and would yield limited cost savings. Under the conservative assumption that genesis Singleton outputs will be rare and occur at most once in a transaction, choosing the simplest approach is preferable.</ref>

* To provide proof of continuation, the lowest bit in the control byte must be set to 1, and the third-to-last witness element after removing the optional annex must publish the next script tree Merkle root. This proves that the continuation output, which must appear at the same index, commits to the same identifier.<ref>'''Why must a continuation output appear at the same index?''' The same-index continuation rule ensures that output validation is O(1) and multiple outputs cannot inherit the same identifier. The rule was chosen for its simplicity and ease of implementation. Providing more flexibility would add complexity to output validation for little real gain.</ref>

A genesis <code>OP_RETURN</code> is 34 bytes and begins with a 32-byte data push:

<source>
OP_RETURN 0x20 [initial script tree Merkle root]
</source>

The <code>OP_RETURN</code> must immediately follow the genesis Singleton output, and the 32-byte hash must equal its initial script tree Merkle root:

<source>
Output N: Genesis P2SG Output
Output N+1: Genesis OP_RETURN
</source>

When spending a Singleton input:
* A witness is provided that resembles a P2TR script-path spend, except the internal key in the control block is replaced with the Singleton identifier.
* Continuation is indicated by the lowest bit of the first byte of the control block, which must be 1 if a continuation and 0 if not.<ref>'''What is the purpose of the continuation bit?''' The continuation bit signals the presence of the next script tree Merkle root in the witness, which must be removed from the witness stack prior to script evaluation. The continuation bit also signals whether the output at the same index is a continuation. This is useful during output validation to quickly identify genesis outputs.</ref>
* If the continuation bit is set, the third-to-last witness element after removing the optional annex must publish the next script tree Merkle root.<ref>'''Why is the next script tree Merkle root not published in the control block?''' In a continuation, the next script tree Merkle root is published in the third-to-last witness element after removing the optional annex. An alternative is to publish the 32-byte next script tree Merkle root in the control block if the continuation bit is set, saving 0.25 vbytes. We choose to forgo this small saving to minimize complexity and simplify reuse of [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP341]] validation logic, as the control block is otherwise nearly identical.</ref>

To illustrate, a Singleton witness provides the following:

<source>
initial stack element 0,
...,
initial stack element N,
next script tree Merkle root, # if the continuation bit is set
leaf script,
control block = [control byte, identifier, 32*m byte Merkle path] # m is the depth of the script in the Merkle tree
</source>

The initial stack elements follow the same rules as P2TR script path spends.<ref>'''Why does P2SG follow nearly the same rules as P2TR script path spends?''' P2SG leverages the battle-tested Tapleaf and Tapscript code already in Bitcoin in order to minimize complexity and implementation risk.</ref>
That is, they place elements on the stack to be evaluated by the leaf script.

The control block is a ''33 + 32 * m'' byte array, where the first byte is the control byte and the next ''32 * m'' bytes are the Merkle path to the leaf script. The top 7 bits of the control byte are used to specify the leaf version, exactly as in P2TR. The lowest bit of the control byte, however, must be 1 if the spend is a continuation and 0 otherwise. P2SG maintains support for the optional annex in the witness (see Specification section below for more details).

The spent Singleton output hash must match the hash of the identifier and the spent script tree Merkle root.

If the continuation bit is set, there must be a Singleton output at the same index whose hash commits to the same identifier and the next script tree Merkle root.

== Specification ==

This section specifies Pay-to-Singleton (P2SG) consensus rules. Validity is defined by exclusion: a block or transaction is valid if no condition exists that marks it failed.

The notation below follows that of [[bip-0341.mediawiki#design|BIP341]].

=== Address Format ===

Singleton outputs use SegWit version 3. This results in mainnet addresses that start with <code>bc1r</code>, following [[bip-0173.mediawiki|BIP 173]]. Bech32m encoding maps version 3 to the prefix <code>r</code>.

Example P2SG address:

<source>
bc1rzmv50jjgxxhww6ve4g5zpewrkjqhr06fyujpm20tuezdlxmfphcqfc80ve
</source>

This commits to a 32-byte hash.

=== ScriptPubKey ===

The <code>scriptPubKey</code> for a Singleton output is:

<source>
OP_3 OP_PUSHBYTES_32 <hash>
</source>

Where:
* <code>OP_3</code> indicates SegWit version 3.
* <code><hash></code> is the 32-byte output hash.

=== Script Validation ===

A Singleton output is a native SegWit output (see [[bip-0141.mediawiki|BIP 141]]) with version 3 and a 32-byte witness program. For the sake of comparison, the language is copied verbatim as much as possible from the script validation section of [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP 341]].

* Let ''q'' be the 32-byte array containing the witness program (the second push in the scriptPubKey) which represents the output hash.
* Fail if the witness stack does not have one or more elements.
* If there are at least two witness elements, and the first byte of the last element is 0x50, this last element is called ''annex a'' and is removed from the witness stack. The annex (or the lack thereof) is always covered by the signature and contributes to transaction weight.
* There must be at least two witness elements left.
** Call the second-to-last stack element ''s'', the script (as defined in [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP 341]]).
** The last stack element is called the control block ''c'', and must have length ''33 + 32 * m'', for a value of ''m'' that is an integer between 0 and 128, inclusive. Fail if it does not have such a length.
** Let ''i = c[1:33]'' be the ''identifier''.
** Let ''v = c[0] & 0xfe'' be the ''leaf version'' (as defined in [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP 341]]).
** Let ''k<sub>0</sub> = hash<sub>TapLeaf</sub>(v || compact_size(size of s) || s)''; also call it the ''tapleaf hash''.
** For ''j'' in ''[0,1,...,m-1]'':
*** Let ''e<sub>j</sub> = c[33+32j:65+32j]''.
*** Let ''k<sub>j+1</sub>'' depend on whether ''k<sub>j</sub> < e<sub>j</sub>'' (lexicographically):
**** If ''k<sub>j</sub> < e<sub>j</sub>'': ''k<sub>j+1</sub> = hash<sub>TapBranch</sub>(k<sub>j</sub> || e<sub>j</sub>)''.
**** If ''k<sub>j</sub> &ge; e<sub>j</sub>'': ''k<sub>j+1</sub> = hash<sub>TapBranch</sub>(e<sub>j</sub> || k<sub>j</sub>)''.
** Let ''r<sub>0</sub> = k<sub>m</sub>''.
** Let ''h<sub>0</sub> = hash<sub>SingletonHash</sub>(i || r<sub>0</sub>)''.
** If ''q &ne; h<sub>0</sub>'', fail.
** If ''c[0] & 0x01 = 1'':
*** There must be at least one witness element left, after removing ''s'' and ''c''.
*** Call the last stack element ''r<sub>1</sub>'', the next script tree Merkle root. Fail if it does not have length 32.
*** Let ''h<sub>1</sub> = hash<sub>SingletonHash</sub>(i || r<sub>1</sub>)''.
*** Fail if there is no version 3 SegWit output at the same index with output hash ''h<sub>1</sub>''.
** Execute the script, according to the script rules specified in BIP 342, using the remaining witness stack elements excluding the script ''s'', the control block ''c'', and the annex ''a'' and next Merkle root ''r<sub>1</sub>'' if present, as initial stack. This implies that for future leaf versions (non-''0xC0'') the execution must succeed.

=== Output Validation ===

Unlike other output types, a Singleton output may not be freely created. A version 3 SegWit output with a 32-byte program created above the activation height must meet the following conditions:

* Let ''q'' be the 32-byte array containing the witness program which represents the output hash.
* If there is an input at index ''n'' that spends a version 3 SegWit output with a 32-byte program and has at least two witness elements:
** Let ''w'' be the witness stack of input ''n''.
** If the first byte of the last element is 0x50, remove the last element.
** Call the control block ''c'' the last remaining stack element.
** If ''c'' is non-empty and ''c[0] & 0x01 = 1'', succeed.
* Fail if the transaction is a coinbase or the output is last in the transaction.
* Let ''s'' be the scriptPubKey of the next output. ''s'' must be 34 bytes and the first byte must be ''OP_RETURN'' and the second byte must be ''0x20''.
* Let ''p'' be the <code>COutPoint</code> of the first input (32-byte hash + 4-byte little-endian).
* Let ''n'' be the output index (4-byte little endian).
* Let ''v'' be the value of output ''n'' (8-byte little endian).
* Call ''r = s[2:34]'' the initial script tree Merkle root.
* Let ''i = hash<sub>SingletonIdentifier</sub>(p || n || v || r)'' be the identifier.
* Let ''h = hash<sub>SingletonHash</sub>(i || r)''.
* Fail if ''q &ne; h''.

=== Common Signature Message Construction ===

The [https://learnmeabitcoin.com/technical/upgrades/taproot/#common-signature-message common signature message] construction for Singleton outputs is exactly the same procedure as defined in [[bip-0342.mediawiki#user-content-Common_Signature_Message_Extension|BIP342 Common Signature Message]].

=== Compatibility with BIP 141 ===

By adhering to the SegWit transaction structure and versioning, Singleton outputs are compatible with existing transaction processing rules. Nodes that do not recognize SegWit version 3 will treat these outputs as anyone-can-spend but generally will not relay or mine such transactions.

== Performance Impact ==

A Singleton spend is as performant as a Taproot script path spend, as the operations to spend a Singleton output are identical, except a tagged hash replaces the tweak to the internal key, and there is an additional tagged hash if the spend is a continuation.

Output validation is the most significant change to block validation, as each transaction output must be checked to determine if it is a genesis Singleton output. While this check is fast and parallelizable, benchmarking has not yet been performed.

== Security ==

Singleton outputs provide the same Tapscript functionality as P2TR outputs. As the output hash is 32 bytes, Singleton outputs also provide 128 bits of collision resistance and 256 bits of preimage resistance.

In addition, the identifier is carefully designed to mitigate the risk of loss of funds due to a chain reorganization. If, for instance, an L2 identifier merely committed to the genesis prevout, a bad actor could reorg the chain and change the unlocking conditions, stealing the funds of users whose deposits would remain valid. Likewise, if the script tree were committed to but not the initial output value, a bad actor could reorg the chain to reduce the initial value while leaving the initial set of depositors intact, allowing those depositors to steal user funds.

By committing to the entire set of initial conditions, we avoid this class of attacks and ensure that L2 behavior is predictable given an identifier.

