== Backward Compatibility ==

Older wallets and nodes that have not been made compatible with SegWit version 3 and P2SG will not understand these outputs. Per [[bip-0350.mediawiki|BIP 350]] older wallets are able to send funds to SegWit version 3 outputs, but only by signing a PSBT transaction with the required <code>OP_RETURN</code> or continuation input.

Users should ensure they are using updated wallets and nodes to receive and validate transactions using Singleton outputs. Pay-to-Singleton is fully compatible with Tapscript and existing Tapscript programs can be used in Singleton outputs without modification. P2SG can also support future scripts with new leaf versions.

== Applications ==

=== Lightning Symmetry without 2x-Delay ===

Lightning Symmetry (LN-Symmetry) suffers from the [https://bitcoinops.org/en/newsletters/2025/01/10/#contract-level-relative-timelocks 2x-delay problem], caused by the relative timelock of the settlement transaction being reset each time an update transaction is confirmed on-chain. This doubles the timelock in HTLCs, reducing the available liquidity and the attractiveness of LN-Symmetry over existing alternatives.

Singleton outputs solve this problem by enabling a contract-level relative timelock at settlement using the [https://delvingbitcoin.org/t/contract-level-relative-timelocks-or-lets-talk-about-ancestry-proofs-and-singletons/1353 proposed] <code>funding->kickoff->update->settle</code> architecture. This can be achieved solely using Singleton outputs and opcodes already in consideration for LN-Symmetry, such as <code>OP_CSFS</code> [[bip-0348.md|BIP 348]] and <code>OP_TEMPLATEHASH</code> [[bip-0446.md|BIP 446]].

The primary change over standard LN-Symmetry is the inclusion of a kickoff transaction, which creates:
* A Singleton output to hold the channel funds for the update path.
* An <code>OP_RETURN</code> that validates the new Singleton output.
* A dust output with a relative timelock that cannot be spent unless the transaction also consumes the aforementioned Singleton output, or a descendant with the same identifier.

The settlement transaction concludes the channel by consuming the Singleton and the timelock simultaneously, thereby enforcing a contract-level relative timelock (anchor outputs not shown):

<pre>
  [ FUNDING TX ]
         │
         ▼
  [ KICKOFF TX ]
  ├── OUTPUT 0: P2TR (Timelock)
  ├── OUTPUT 1: P2SG (Update Path Funds)
  └── OUTPUT 2: OP_RETURN (Singleton Genesis Proof)
         │                    │
         │                    ▼
         │             [ UPDATE TX 1 ]
         │                    │
         │                    ▼
 (Consumes Timelock)   [ UPDATE TX N ]
         │                    │
         │                    ▼
         │             [ SETTLEMENT TX ]
         └──────▶ (Input 0: Kickoff Timelock Output)
                  (Input 1: Update N P2SG Output)
</pre>

Sibling commitments can be emulated by committing to zero-value anyone-can-spend outputs that enforce either genesis or continuation. For instance, the settlement transaction could include:
* A Singleton output that continues the Singleton created in the kickoff transaction
* A genesis Singleton output whose identifier commits to the timelock being spent in the first input (followed by the necessary <code>OP_RETURN</code>).

In this manner, a presigned transaction can commit to both inputs solely by committing to the outputs of the settlement transaction.

While functional, this approach is inefficient and can be improved using [[bip-XXXX-op-singleton.md|OP_SINGLETON]] in the timelock output, which commits to the channel Singleton being consumed at input index 1. This makes the timelock signature-less and eliminates the first P2SG output in the settlement transaction while precluding a premature spend in an update transaction. Eliminating the second Singleton output, however, would require a modification to [[bip-0446.md|OP_TEMPLATEHASH]] or a new opcode that allows the settlement hash to commit to a sibling prevout.

Finally, note that a Singleton output is not used in the funding transaction. This keeps cooperative channel closures indistinguishable from other P2TR spends.

The primary drawback is that the funding transaction cannot commit via [[bip-0446.md|OP_TEMPLATEHASH]] to the kickoff transaction without creating a hash cycle, requiring additional interactivity at setup. To eliminate this interactivity, an entirely new opcode would be required, which is out-of-scope of this BIP.

=== Non-Interactive Bridging ===

A common problem faced by proposals like [https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/068 ShieldedCSV] is how to bridge user funds to the L2. For on-chain efficiency, L2 funds are typically held in a single UTXO, which creates state contention if multiple users try to add funds at the same time. Users must either sign a deposit each time one fails, cooperate with other users, or swap with a sophisticated liquidity provider using HTLCs, which is capital inefficient and expensive for large transfers.

Singleton outputs solve this problem by creating a stable identifier unique to the L2 that users can delegate to. Using [[bip-XXXX-op-singleton.md|OP_SINGLETON]] with [[bip-0348.md|OP_CSFS]], users can delegate their UTXO to the L2 while committing to spending conditions that the L2 must respect (e.g., via an adaptor signature). In this manner, a user can make a deposit directly to the L2 without dealing with state contention or coordinating with other users. Deposits would remain valid indefinitely, and transaction builders would compete to aggregate pending deposits and produce completed transactions, capturing a portion of the aggregate fee. This creates a competitive market for L2 inclusion and eliminates the interactivity requirements of HTLC swaps.

== Related Work ==

The singleton concept has been discussed a number of times in the Bitcoin community. For instance, [https://delvingbitcoin.org/t/contract-level-relative-timelocks-or-lets-talk-about-ancestry-proofs-and-singletons/1353 this Delving thread] discusses the use of ancestor proofs to enforce contract-level relative timelocks in Lightning Symmetry. Participants discuss the challenges of implementing this capability, suggesting the use of opcodes like <code>OP_CAT</code> [[bip-0347.mediawiki|BIP 347]] to implement recursive covenants that validate the parent and grandparent transactions, an idea previously discussed [https://gnusha.org/pi/bitcoindev/20220310064717.GA7597@erisian.com.au/ on the mailing list].

While recursive introspection is a viable way to enforce singleton behavior, it has several drawbacks over P2SG's approach. For one, it is considerably more expensive to implement, requiring a large witness, a complex script, and new introspection opcodes. The approach also imposes an asymmetric cost on users, where the amortized cost of interacting with the singleton could rise considerably if the parent or grandparent transactions have many more inputs or outputs than usual. The only conceivable way to eliminate this asymmetry is with a zero-knowledge proof, which adds considerable cost and complexity. Moreover, a script-based approach makes it more challenging to implement standardized introspection of the singleton identifier.

[https://github.com/JohnLaw2/btc-iids/blob/main/iids14.pdf Inherited IDs] is another proposal that offers ancestor-proof capabilities. Unlike P2SG, however, the Inherited ID proposal requires nodes to store additional state outside the UTXO set. This adds considerable complexity to validation and is incompatible with Utreexo. In addition, the proposal lacks support for stable identifiers, complicating usage.

